! edge — AS65300  (Arista EOS)
!
! Enterprise WAN edge router — dual-homed to two ISPs.
! Outbound: prefer isp1 via higher local-preference (200 vs 100)
! Inbound:  AS-path prepend on isp2 advertisements to attract traffic via isp1
! Internal: OSPF area 0 to core1/core2, default-information originate always
!
! Links:
!   Ethernet1: 203.0.113.2/30  (to isp1 — primary WAN)
!   Ethernet2: 203.0.114.2/30  (to isp2 — backup WAN)
!   Ethernet3: 10.255.1.1/30   (to core1)
!   Ethernet4: 10.255.2.1/30   (to core2)
!   Loopback0: 10.0.0.1/32
!
hostname edge
!
interface Loopback0
   ip address 10.0.0.1/32
!
interface Ethernet1
   description to-isp1-primary  203.0.113.0/30
   no switchport
   ip address 203.0.113.2/30
   no shutdown
!
interface Ethernet2
   description to-isp2-backup  203.0.114.0/30
   no switchport
   ip address 203.0.114.2/30
   no shutdown
!
interface Ethernet3
   description to-core1  10.255.1.0/30
   no switchport
   ip address 10.255.1.1/30
   no shutdown
!
interface Ethernet4
   description to-core2  10.255.2.0/30
   no switchport
   ip address 10.255.2.1/30
   no shutdown
!
ip routing
!
! ================================================
! edge  —  Capstone Tasks
!
! Role: Dual-homed WAN edge — BGP policy + OSPF default propagation
!
! Task 1 — eBGP to isp1 (primary) with LP 200 inbound
!   Set local-preference 200 on all routes received from isp1.
!   This makes isp1 the preferred exit for all outbound traffic.
!   Hint:
!     route-map LP-HIGH permit 10
!        set local-preference 200
!
! Task 2 — eBGP to isp2 (backup) with LP 100 inbound + AS-path prepend outbound
!   Set local-preference 100 on routes from isp2 (cold standby outbound).
!   Prepend AS 65300 three times on advertisements to isp2 to make isp1
!   appear as the shorter AS path for inbound traffic from the internet.
!   Hint:
!     route-map LP-LOW permit 10
!        set local-preference 100
!     ip prefix-list OWN-PREFIXES seq 5 permit 198.51.100.0/24
!     route-map PREPEND-ISP2 permit 10
!        match ip address prefix-list OWN-PREFIXES
!        set as-path prepend 65300 65300 65300
!     route-map PREPEND-ISP2 permit 20
!
! Task 3 — router bgp 65300 with dual eBGP sessions
!   Configure BGP, peer with both ISPs, apply route-maps, originate
!   the enterprise public prefix via a black-hole static backing route.
!   Hint:
!     ip route 198.51.100.0/24 Null0
!     router bgp 65300
!        router-id 10.0.0.1
!        no bgp ebgp-requires-policy
!        neighbor 203.0.113.1 remote-as 65100
!        neighbor 203.0.113.1 description isp1-primary
!        neighbor 203.0.113.1 route-map LP-HIGH in
!        neighbor 203.0.114.1 remote-as 65200
!        neighbor 203.0.114.1 description isp2-backup
!        neighbor 203.0.114.1 route-map LP-LOW in
!        neighbor 203.0.114.1 route-map PREPEND-ISP2 out
!        address-family ipv4
!           neighbor 203.0.113.1 activate
!           neighbor 203.0.114.1 activate
!           network 198.51.100.0/24
!
! Task 4 — OSPF area 0 + default-information originate
!   Run OSPF on Ethernet3/4 (internal links). WAN interfaces stay passive.
!   Inject a default route into OSPF so core routers learn the internet default.
!   Hint:
!     router ospf 1
!        router-id 10.0.0.1
!        passive-interface default
!        no passive-interface Ethernet3
!        no passive-interface Ethernet4
!        network 10.0.0.1/32 area 0.0.0.0
!        network 10.255.1.0/30 area 0.0.0.0
!        network 10.255.2.0/30 area 0.0.0.0
!        default-information originate always
! ================================================
!
end
